1	//! # The machine: vx86 CPU + memory + the Rust core routines
2	//!
3	//! ## Execution model
4	//!
5	//! The machine executes vx86 instructions one at a time.  When EIP lands in
6	//! the gateway region it runs the corresponding Rust routine (a [`Gate`])
7	//! instead of decoding bytes.  A gateway is entered exactly like a native
8	//! routine — by `call`, `jmp` or `ret` — and finishes with one of:
9	//!
10	//! * `Ret` — pop the return address, as the assembly routine's `ret` did;
11	//! * `Jump(a)` — continue at `a` (a tail jump, e.g. `mclass: jmp ebx`);
12	//! * `Call(a, k)` — push gateway `k` as the return address and go to `a`.
13	//!   This is how Rust routines call back into Forth *without* using the Rust
14	//!   stack: the rest of the routine is the continuation gateway `k`;
15	//! * `NeedInput` — stay at this gateway and return to the host until input
16	//!   arrives; re-entering the gateway retries.
17	//!
18	//! Because every routine on the outer-interpreter path (interpret, query,
19	//! eval, include, catch) is written this way, the VM can stop after any
20	//! instruction and resume later, which is what a browser host requires.
21	//! A few rarely-hooked callbacks (`do_cr`, `prompt`, `iterate`, user-defined
22	//! `emit`/`type`) use [`Machine::call_nested`] instead; see its notes.
